{%- if coverage_model == "graph" %}

Record all discoveries into a file at the repository root named `UI_GRAPH.json`. This file is your durable working memory and must remain accurate for the whole run. If your context is ever summarized, compacted, or truncated, re-read this file FIRST to recover the full inventory before continuing.

Use this JSON structure:

```json
{
  "version": "2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "app_name": "<application name>",
    "total_states": <total node count>,
    "explored": <nodes with status explored>,
    "skipped": <nodes with status skipped>
  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "<route or route#qualifier>",
      "route": "<URL path or pattern>",
      "label": "<what is observable in this state>",
      "section": "<feature area grouping>",
      "status": "discovered|explored|skipped",
      "auth_required": true|false,
      "layout": "<shared layout id if applicable>",
      "actions": ["<available user actions in this state>"],
      "notes": "<optional, < 80 chars>"
    }
  ],
  "edges": [
    {
      "from": "<source state id>",
      "to": "<target state id>",
      "trigger": "<UI element: type, visible text or label, identifier>",
      "guard": "<precondition, if not always available>"
    }
  ],
  "groups": {
    "auth_zones": [
      { "id": "zone:<name>", "label": "<description>", "node_ids": ["..."] }
    ],
    "layouts": [
      { "id": "layout:<name>", "label": "<description>", "node_ids": ["..."] }
    ]
  }
}
```

**What is a state (node)?**

A node represents a distinct UI state — an observable configuration of the interface that determines what the user can see, interact with, and reach next. Multiple states can exist under the same URL route.

Create a **new** node when at least one of the following differs from any existing node:
- **Rendered interface** — meaningful visible components differ (e.g., a modal is now open, a tab panel has switched)
- **Action availability** — an action appears, disappears, becomes enabled or disabled
- **Reachability** — different subsequent states or workflows are reachable from here
- **Behavior** — performing the same action produces a different effect or destination
- **Interaction context** — focus, selection, authorization, or mode changes how the user can interact

Do NOT create new nodes for: loading spinners, skeleton screens, animation frames, timestamps updating, randomized IDs, or minor content changes that do not alter available actions or reachable states.

**Node ID format:**
- Route-level states: use the route path as ID (e.g., `/login`, `/dashboard`, `/users/:id`)
- Sub-states within a route: append `#qualifier` (e.g., `/settings#security-tab`, `/login#mfa-input`, `/projects#create-modal`)

**Edge triggers:**

Each edge describes the specific UI element that causes the transition. The `trigger` field should reference the most stable identifier available, in order of preference: `data-testid`, `aria-label`, visible text content, or element role + position. Examples:
- `button "Sign in" [data-testid=login-submit]`
- `link "Dashboard" in sidebar nav`
- `tab "Security" [role=tab]`
- `auto: redirect on 401` (for automatic/system-triggered transitions)

Set `guard` only when the transition is conditionally available (requires auth, specific role, feature flag, or data state).

**Rules:**
- List the `actions` array on each node — these are the user-facing actions available in that state. This makes state identity auditable: two nodes should have different action sets.
- Group protected states into `auth_zones`; group states sharing a shell/layout into `layouts`.
- Update `metadata` counts after each modification.
- Always update the file by reading the existing JSON, modifying in memory, and writing the full file back. Never surgically edit JSON with sed or awk.
- After each write, validate: `python3 -c "import json; json.load(open('UI_GRAPH.json'))"`
- Keep `label` and `notes` fields terse (< 80 characters).
- The task is NOT complete while any node has status `"discovered"` — continue until every node is `"explored"` or `"skipped"`.
{%- else %}

Record all discoveries into a file at the repository root named `UI_COVERAGE.md`. This file is your durable working memory and must remain accurate for the whole run. If your context is ever summarized, compacted, or truncated, re-read this file FIRST to recover the full inventory before continuing. Use this format:

```
# UI Coverage (Discovery: <discovered>/<explored>)
Legend: [ ] found but not yet explored  -  [x] explored and confirmed  -  [~] unreachable (reason)

## <Section / Feature area>
- [ ] <route or flow> - <short note: key interactions, auth required, states, etc.>
```

Keep entries terse (route + one-line note). When you explore a page and confirm it exists, mark it `[x]`. If a page is unreachable (broken link, requires external service, etc.), mark it `[~]` with a reason. The task is NOT complete while any entry remains `[ ]` — continue exploring until every discovered item is either `[x]` or `[~]`.
{%- endif %}

Do NOT modify production code under any circumstances. Do NOT write any test files. Do NOT install Playwright or any test framework. Your only output is the `{{ tracker_file }}` file.

In the final response, summarize: total pages/flows discovered, how many confirmed vs unreachable, which sections of the app have the richest UI surface, and any areas that may need special handling (auth, external APIs, dynamic content).
